   "answer": "## Results\n\n**Method:** I read the case table. It has 75 rows, one row for each case. The table holds percents, so no image analysis ran. The scoring tool computed the H-scores from the four percents of each case. The H-score formula is 1 x percent of 1+ cells, plus 2 x percent of 2+ cells, plus 3 x percent of 3+ cells. The range is 0 to 300. The agreement tool gave the Spearman correlations, the weighted kappa and the ICC. The pathologist is the reference scorer. A script step also read the source table to check two values.\n\n**Number of cases (n) for each marker:**\n- 5T4: 21 cases\n- P-cadherin: 30 cases\n- PD-L1: 24 cases\n- Total: 75 cases. One case is one row. The table does not say if a case is one patient or one animal.\n\n**H-score of PD-L1 case 1:** 185. The percents in the table were 0 % at 0, 20 % at 1+, 75 % at 2+ and 5 % at 3+. The calculation is 1 x 20 + 2 x 75 + 3 x 5. The pathologist_h column in the source table also gives 185.0 for this case. A script step read this value from the file.\n\n**Spearman correlation with the pathologist H-score:**\n\n| Marker | n | QuPath rho (p) | HALO rho (p) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5T4 | 21 | 0.789 (p = 0.00002) | 0.750 (p = 0.00009) |\n| P-cadherin | 30 | 0.392 (p = 0.032) | 0.495 (p = 0.005) |\n| PD-L1 | 24 | 0.739 (p = 0.00004) | 0.701 (p = 0.0001) |\n\n**P-cadherin agreement, pathologist against QuPath (n = 30 cases):**\n- Weighted kappa, quadratic weights, cut points at 100 and 200: 0.011. The 95 % interval is −0.109 to 0.128.\n- ICC(2,1): 0.149. The 95 % interval is −0.091 to 0.437.\n- Mean difference, pathologist minus QuPath: +115.2 H-score units. A script step gave the same value, +115.15. The limits of agreement are −32.7 to 263.0.\n- The positive sign means the pathologist scores are about 115 units higher than the QuPath scores on average.\n\n## What the results show\n\n- The correlations are high for 5T4 and PD-L1. The P-cadherin correlations are moderate.\n- For P-cadherin, the QuPath scores agree poorly with the pathologist. The kappa and ICC are close to zero. The pathologist scores are about 115 units higher on average.\n- A correlation does not show agreement. The high correlations for 5T4 and PD-L1 do not show that the scores match.\n\n## Uncertain\n\n- The intervals are wide. The P-cadherin kappa and ICC intervals include zero.\n- The kappa depends on the cut points. The scientist chose the cut points at 100 and 200 in the request. Other cut points give other kappa values. I did not test other cut points.\n- The 2+ threshold (0.4) and the 3+ threshold (0.6) that the scientist answered do not apply to this case table. They apply to image analysis, which did not run.\n\n## Waiting for the scientist\n\n- Confirm that a row is one patient or one animal. The replication unit sets the n for each group.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Region to score: whole · Threshold for 1+ (DAB optical density): 0.2 · Threshold for 2+ (DAB optical density): 0.4 · Threshold for 3+ (DAB optical density): 0.6 · Smallest number of cells for a score: 0 · Weights of the kappa: quadratic · Form of the intraclass correlation: ICC2 · Cut points that make categories from a continuous score: 100,200.",
